*norg-diagram.norg*  Text-rendered diagrams for Neorg

* norg-diagram                                                 *norg-diagram*

  Renders {** Renderers}[mermaid, d2 and graph-easy] diagrams as text inside
  `.norg` buffers, using virtual lines.

  No image protocol is involved, so diagrams work over ssh, inside tmux, and
  in any terminal. They scroll, fold and search like the rest of the buffer.

  NOTE: If you have `core.concealer` enabled, run `:Neorg toggle-concealer`
  to see the raw markup in this file.

** Contents                                           *norg-diagram-contents*

   ~ {** Requirements}
   ~ {** Usage}
   ~ {** Renderers}
   ~ {** Directives}
   ~ {** Configuration}
   ~ {** Commands}
   ~ {** Troubleshooting}

** Requirements                                   *norg-diagram-requirements*

   - Neovim 0.11 or later, for `conceal_lines`
   - Neorg, with the `norg` treesitter parser installed
   - At least one renderer binary on your `PATH`, or configured by absolute
     path (see {** Renderers})

   Run `:checkhealth norg-diagram` to verify all of the above.

** Usage                                                 *norg-diagram-usage*

   Write a diagram in a code block tagged with the renderer's name:

   @code mermaid
   graph LR
   A[Index] --> B[Projects]
   @end

   The rendered diagram appears above or below the block, and the source is concealed
   while `core.concealer` is active. Edit the source and it re-renders when
   you leave insert mode or stop typing.

   Rendering is cached by source, renderer and window width, so re-rendering
   an unchanged diagram costs nothing.

*** Setup                                                *norg-diagram-setup*

    @code lua
    require("norg-diagram").setup({
      renderers = {
        mermaid = { bin = "mermaid-ascii" },
        d2 = { bin = "d2", layout = "tala" },
      },
      classes = {
        service = "Function",
        store = "Constant",
      },
    })
    @end

    Every option is described under {** Configuration}.

** Renderers                                         *norg-diagram-renderers*

   Each renderer is an entry in the `renderers` table, keyed by the tag you
   write after `@code`. Only `bin` normally needs setting; it may be a name
   on `PATH` or an absolute path.

*** mermaid                                            *norg-diagram-mermaid*

    Uses {https://github.com/AlexanderGrooff/mermaid-ascii}[mermaid-ascii].
    Flowcharts and sequence diagrams. Supports per-node colors through
    mermaid's `:::` class syntax (see {*** Classes}).

    Options:
    - `border_padding`   space between a label and its border (default 1)
    - `padding_x`        horizontal space between nodes (default 5)
    - `padding_y`        vertical space between nodes (default 5)
    - `coords`           draw debug coordinates
    - `verbose`          verbose renderer output

    Honours the global `max_width` (see {*** Display}).

*** d2                                                      *norg-diagram-d2*

    Uses {https://d2lang.com}[d2] with `--stdout-format ascii`. Requires d2
    0.7.1 or later; 0.9.0 or later for the `tala` layout; 0.9.0 or later is recommended as it is much faster.

    Options:
    - `layout`      `dagro` | `elk` | `tala`; nil uses d2's default
    - `ascii_mode`  `extended` (Unicode) or `standard` (ASCII)

    NOTE: d2's ASCII renderer supports no styles, so colors come from
    {*** Color directives} rather than from the diagram source.

    NOTE: `standard` mode draws boxes with `+` and `|`, which box colouring
    cannot detect. Keep `extended` unless you need pure ASCII.

*** graph-easy                                      *norg-diagram-graph-easy*

    Uses {https://metacpan.org/pod/Graph::Easy}[Graph::Easy]. Good for state machines and automata:
    it handles self-loops and edge labels better than
    the others.

    Options:
    - `as`   `boxart` (Unicode) or `ascii`

    NOTE: combining `origin`/`offset` with `flow` can make Graph::Easy hang.
    Prefer `flow` alone and let it place the nodes.

** Directives                                       *norg-diagram-directives*

   Directives are comment lines inside a diagram block. They are stripped
   before the source reaches the renderer, so they never affect layout.

   Use the comment character of the language you are writing: `%%` for
   mermaid, `#` for d2 and graph-easy. Both forms are accepted everywhere.

*** Color directives                                    *norg-diagram-color*

    @code
    #color <label> <color>
    #color <label> <color> box
    @end

    Colors the text of `<label>` wherever it appears in the rendered output,
    or the whole box containing it when `box` is given.

    `<color>` may be:
    - a palette name: `red`, `green`, `yellow`, `blue`, `magenta`, `cyan`,
      `white`, `black`, and their `bright_` variants. These resolve against
      your colorscheme's terminal colors, so they follow your theme.
    - a highlight group name, such as `DiagnosticError`
    - a literal `#rrggbb` value

    Both forms may be given for the same label, so a box and its label can
    take different colors:

    @code d2
    api: API
    db: Postgres
    api -> db
    #color API red box
    #color API green
    @end

*** Classes                                            *norg-diagram-classes*

    For mermaid only. Nodes tagged with `:::name` take the color mapped to
    that class name. No `classDef` line is needed; one is generated.

    @code mermaid
    graph LR
    api[API]:::service --> db[Postgres]:::store
    @end

    Class names that match a palette name resolve to that theme color.
    Others are mapped through the `classes` option, and anything left over
    takes a color from a built-in palette.

    A class color always wins over a `#color` directive for the same label.

*** Link directives                                      *norg-diagram-links*

    @code norg
    #link <label> {:some/file:}
    #link <label> {:some/file:** Heading}
    @end

    Marks a node as a link to a Neorg target. In the inline render the label
    is highlighted as a link; to follow it, open the diagram in a float with
    `:NorgDiagramFloat` (see {** Commands}).

    Links cannot be followed inline, because virtual text has no cursor
    position. The float holds the same diagram as real text, with Neorg's
    own link handling applied.

** Configuration                                        *norg-diagram-config*

*** Display                                            *norg-diagram-display*

    - `max_width`      `"window"` fits the window, `"auto"` lets the renderer
                       decide, a number sets an explicit column count, `nil`
                       omits the limit (default `"window"`)
    - `conceal`        hide the source block (default `true`)
    - `fold`           close the block's fold, so the cursor steps over it in
                       one motion (default `true`)
    - `position`       `"replace"` hangs the diagram where the block sits,
      `"after"` puts it below the block (default
      `"replace"`)
    - `concealcursor`  value set for norg buffers; `""` leaves it alone
                       (default `"nvic"`)

    Concealing follows `:Neorg toggle-concealer`: turning Neorg's concealer
    off reveals diagram sources too.

*** Highlighting                                    *norg-diagram-highlights*

    - `hl`        group for uncoloured diagram text (default `Comment`)
    - `edge_hl`   group for box borders and connectors (default `Comment`)
    - `err_hl`    group for renderer errors (default `DiagnosticError`)
    - `link_hl`   group for linked labels
                  (default `@neorg.links.description`)
    - `classes`   map of class name to highlight group, e.g.
                  `{ service = "Function" }`
    - `box_color` allow `box` directives to colour whole rectangles
                  (default `true`)

    Setting `edge_hl` to something dimmer than `hl` makes labels stand
    forward and usually reads better than a uniformly coloured diagram.

*** Behaviour                                        *norg-diagram-behaviour*

    - `debounce_ms`  delay before re-rendering after a change (default 300)
    - `timeout_ms`   kill a renderer that has not finished (default 5000)
    - `enabled`      render at all (default `true`)

** Commands                                           *norg-diagram-commands*

   - `:NorgDiagramRender`      re-render the current buffer
   - `:NorgDiagramToggle`      turn rendering on or off
   - `:NorgDiagramClearCache`  drop cached renders and re-render
   - `:NorgDiagramFloat`       open the diagram under the cursor in a float, where `<Tab>` cycles linked nodes and `<CR>` follows one

** Troubleshooting                                   *norg-diagram-troubles*

   $ Nothing renders
   Run `:checkhealth norg-diagram`. The usual causes are a missing renderer
   binary or a missing `norg` treesitter parser.

   $ The source is visible alongside the diagram
   `conceallevel` must be 2. Neorg normally sets this; check with
   `:setlocal conceallevel?`.

   $ The block reveals itself as the cursor passes over it
   `concealcursor` must include `n`. Set the `concealcursor` option, or set
   it yourself for norg buffers.

   $ Colors do not follow my colorscheme
   Palette names resolve through `vim.g.terminal_color_*`. A colorscheme
   that does not set those falls back to a fixed group per name.

   $ A diagram is cut off at the right edge
   Virtual lines do not wrap. Use `max_width = "window"`, or switch the
   diagram to a vertical layout.

   $ Changing a renderer option does nothing
   Renders are cached. Run `:NorgDiagramClearCache`.

